"Retrieve What’s Missing: Coverage-Maximizing Retrieval for Consistent Long Video Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.02479v1)\nciteturn247view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2606.02479v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 396\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 Preliminaries L19:   5. cite9†4 Method L20:     1. cite10†4.1 Target-view coverage maps as 3D memory evidence L21:     2. cite11†4.2 Residual coverage gain for non-redundant retrieval L22:     3. cite12†4.3 Scalable depth estimation via sliding-window caching L23:   6. cite13†5 Experiments L24:     1. cite14†5.1 Geometric consistency evaluation L25:     2. cite15†5.2 Retrieval cost comparison L26:     3. cite16†5.3 Analysis L27:   7. cite17†6 Conclusion L28:   8. cite18†References L29:   9. cite19†A Experimental Details L30:     1. cite20†A.1 Baseline Setup L31:       1. cite21†DFoT. L32:       2. cite22†WorldMem. L33:       3. cite23†VMem. L34:     2. cite24†A.2 Model training details L35:     3. cite25†A.3 Evaluation protocol L36:       1. cite26†RealEstate10K. L37:       2. cite27†DL3DV10K. L38:     4. cite28†A.4 GT Pose Alignment for VGGT-based Geometry Estimation L39:   10. cite29†B Additional Results L40:     1. cite30†B.1 Qualitative Results L41:       1. cite31†RealEstate10K. L42:       2. cite32†DL3DV10K. L43:     2. cite33†B.2 Quantitative Results L44:       1. cite34†Scalability as video length increases. L45:       2. cite35†Robustness to inaccurate geometry. L46:   11. cite36†C Limitations L47:   12. cite37†D Future work L48:   13. cite38†E Broader impacts L49: cite39†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L50: \nL51: arXiv:2606.02479v1 [cs.CV] 01 Jun 2026\nL52: # Retrieve What’s Missing: Coverage-Maximizing Retrieval for Consistent Long Video Generation\nL53: \nL54: Minseok Joo Affiliation: Korea University Email: wlgkcjf87@korea.ac.kr    Dogyun Park Affiliation: Korea University Email: gg933@korea.ac.kr    Taehoon Lee Affiliation: KAIST Email: dlxogns183@kaist.ac.kr    Kyujin Lee Affiliation: KAIST Email: kyujinlee@kaist.ac.kr    Hyunwoo J. Kim ^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author. Affiliation: KAIST Email: hyunwoojkim@kaist.ac.kr\nL55: ###### Abstract\nL56: Maintaining long-term geometric consistency remains challenging for long-horizon autoregressive video generation. Memory-augmented generative models address this by retrieving historical frames, but their effectiveness depends on two key design choices: what 3D-geometric evidence should represent past observations, and how memory frames should be selected from this evidence.\nL57: Existing methods often rely on camera poses or field-of-view overlap, which are lightweight but too coarse to reason about pixel-wise visibility, or use explicit 3D reconstruction, which provides fine-grained evidence but is costly to maintain over long rollouts. We propose Cov erage-Maximizing R etrieval-A ugmented G eneration (CovRAG), a depth-based memory retrieval framework that uses pretrained 3D priors to construct a target-view coverage map as lightweight 3D memory evidence.\nL58: For frame selection, CovRAG maximizes residual coverage gain, iteratively retrieving frames that explain target-view regions not covered by the current context or previously selected memories. To improve scalability in long-video generation, we introduce sliding-window depth caching for efficient geometry estimation. Experiments on RealEstate10K and DL3DV10K show that CovRAG improves long-horizon geometric consistency while maintaining low latency compared to baselines.\nL59: cite40†Image: [Uncaptioned image] Figure 1: CovRAG improves consistency under occlusion and out-of-frame motion. CovRAG preserves reappearing content and achieves a better consistency–efficiency trade-off than FoV-based retrieval (WorldMem) and explicit 3D memory (VMem).\nL60: ## 1 Introduction\nL61: Simulating the visual world is a central goal of computer vision. Long-horizon video generation that behaves as a consistent 3D scene, rather than a sequence of locally plausible views, is essential for world models that support robotic simulation cite41†Wen et al. (2024) ; cite42†Wolf et al. (2025) ; cite43†Yu et al. (2025e) ; cite44†Zhu et al. (2025) , 3D content creation cite45†Yu et al. (2024) ; cite46†He et al. (2025) ; cite47†Bai et al. (2025) ; cite48†Gao et al. (2024) ; cite49†Yu et al. (2025d) ; cite50†Yu et al.\nL62: (2025a) , and interactive game engines cite51†Feng et al. (2025) ; cite52†Yu et al. (2025c) ; cite53†Valevski et al. (2025) ; cite54†Bruce et al. (2024) . Despite this progress, maintaining long-term geometric consistency remains challenging. Most autoregressive video diffusion models generate videos within a limited temporal context window and thus tend to lose structural and semantic details beyond it.\nL63: When the camera revisits a previously observed region, the model may fail to reproduce geometry and appearance consistent with the earlier observation, leading to spatial drift, flickering, and inconsistent scene structure. A promising direction is memory-augmented video generation cite55†Xiao et al. (2025) ; cite56†Yu et al. (2025b) ; cite57†Li et al. (2025) ; cite58†Chen et al. (2025) ; cite59†Henschel et al. (2025) ; cite60†Gao et al.\nL64: (2026) , which stores historical frames or states and retrieves a subset of them to condition future generation. This approach is attractive because it sidesteps the cost of extending the generator’s context: scaling retrieval is far more tractable than scaling the model’s context length, and retrieval can be improved without retraining the generator. However, the effectiveness of memory-augmented generation depends critically on the retrieval mechanism.\nL65: In particular, retrieval must answer two key questions: (i) What form of 3D-aware evidence best captures the geometric relevance of past observations? and (ii) How should frames be selected based on this evidence?\nL66: Existing methods make undesirable trade-offs for the first question. Some approaches, such as WorldMem cite55†Xiao et al. (2025) , use camera poses or field-of-view (FoV) overlap as lightweight 3D-aware evidence. This is efficient and easy to scale, but it provides only a coarse view-level proxy for geometric relevance.\nL67: FoV overlap does not reveal which target-view pixels are actually visible from a memory frame, cannot reason about occlusion, and cannot determine whether a past observation provides evidence about locally occluded or out of frame in the context frames. At the other extreme, methods such as VMem cite57†Li et al. (2025) construct explicit 3D scene representations and render memory evidence into the target view.\nL68: Such explicit reconstruction provides fine-grained geometric information but incurs substantial computational and engineering costs, especially when the scene representation must be maintained over long autoregressive horizons. Thus, existing memory evidence is often either too coarse to support reliable visibility reasoning or too expensive to scale efficiently. On the second question, the standard strategy scores each candidate frame independently and retrieves the top-$n$ frames.\nL69: This finds frames individually relevant to the target view, but offers no guarantee that the retrieved set is collectively informative. Multiple frames may redundantly explain the same region, while other regions remain unsupported. This redundancy is especially harmful under tight context-length constraints and compute budgets.\nL70: Thus, we propose Coverage-Maximizing Retrieval (CovRAG), a depth-based retrieval framework, illustrated in Figure cite61†2 , to address both limitations. For memory evidence, CovRAG uses depth and camera poses predicted by a pretrained 3D prior model cite62†Wang et al. (2025a) to construct a target-view coverage map.\nL71: Concretely, each historical frame is warped to the target viewpoint using its predicted depth and camera pose, and the resulting target-view coverage map indicates which target-view pixels are geometrically supported by that frame. This depth-based evidence provides a practical middle ground between FoV overlap and explicit 3D reconstruction: it is sufficiently fine-grained to support pixel-wise visibility reasoning, yet lightweight enough to avoid maintaining a globally reconstructed scene.\nL72: For frame selection, CovRAG retrieves frames to maximize residual coverage gain, the number of currently uncovered pixels each new frame would explain. This yields a compact, complementary memory set rather than a redundant top-$n$ selection. To keep this approach scalable over long rollouts, we introduce a sliding-window depth-caching strategy that confines inference to a fixed window and reuses cached depth maps thereafter, keeping inference cost nearly constant as the generated sequence grows.\nL73: We evaluate CovRAG on top of DFoT cite63†Song et al. (2025) on RealEstate10K cite64†Zhou et al. (2018) and DL3DV10K cite65†Ling et al. (2024) , which together cover diverse 3D scenes with varied camera trajectories and substantial spatial extent. Compared with memory-retrieval baselines, CovRAG reduces the geometric consistency error measured by MEt3R by up to 29%, while achieving up to 5$\\times$ lower latency than a 3D reconstruction-based method.\nL74: These results indicate that CovRAG is an effective and efficient retrieval method for 3D-consistent video generation.\nL75: Contributions. Our contributions are summarized as follows:\nL76: \nL77:   * •\nL78: \nL79: We propose Coverage-Maximizing Retrieval (CovRAG), a depth-based memory-retrieval framework to improve geometric consistency in long-horizon autoregressive video generation.\nL80: \nL81:   * •\nL82: We introduce target-view coverage maps, a lightweight 3D-aware memory evidence representation that uses pretrained 3D priors to estimate which target-view pixels are geometrically supported by each historical frame, enabling pixel-level visibility reasoning without explicit 3D reconstruction.\nL83: \nL84:   * •\nL85: We introduce residual coverage gain, an iterative frame-selection criterion that retrieves memory frames covering currently uncovered target-view regions, reducing redundancy among retrieved frames and encouraging complementary geometric evidence.\nL86: \nL87:   * •\nL88: We enable scalable depth-based coverage retrieval with sliding-window depth caching, which estimates depth only within a fixed local rollout window and reuses cached depth maps for later coverage computation, keeping geometry inference cost nearly constant as the video grows.\nL89: ## 2 Related Work\nL90: cite66†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of diffusion video generation with CovRAG. During autoregressive video generation, CovRAG augments the diffusion model with retrieved historical frames that provide complementary geometric evidence for the target view. It identifies regions already supported by the temporal context and retrieves additional memories (denoted by red and blue regions) that cover the remaining target-view regions, enabling more consistent long-horizon generation.\nL91: Long-horizon autoregressive video generation. Diffusion-based video generation has rapidly improved in visual fidelity and temporal coherence cite67†Ho et al. (2020) ; cite68†Song et al. (2021) ; cite69†Kong et al. (2024) ; cite70†Yang et al. (2025) ; cite71†Wan et al. (2025) . To extend generation beyond fixed length, two complementary strategies have emerged. Context-extension methods scale the temporal window directly through autoregressive or streaming mechanisms cite63†Song et al. (2025) ; cite72†Chen et al.\nL92: (2024) ; cite73†Yin et al. (2025) ; cite74†Huang et al. (2025) ; cite75†Lin et al. (2025) , generating each frame conditioned on a short history. While effective for short- to medium-term horizons, the cost of attending to longer histories grows rapidly, and the effective context window remains too small to retain information across scene revisits. Memory-augmentation methods, discussed next, instead decouple temporal coverage from context length by retrieving informative past frames on demand.\nL93: Our work follows this second strategy and focuses on improving the retrieval mechanism, which we identify as the primary determinant of long-horizon geometric consistency.\nL94: Memory-augmented video generation. A growing body of work incorporates external memory into video diffusion models cite55†Xiao et al. (2025) ; cite56†Yu et al. (2025b) ; cite57†Li et al. (2025) ; cite60†Gao et al. (2026) ; cite76†Cai et al. (2026) ; cite77†Liu et al. (2024) ; cite78†Wang and Agapito (2025) ; cite79†Ren et al. (2025) ; cite80†Zhang et al. (2025) , conditioning generation on a small set of retrieved historical observations rather than the entire past.\nL95: Existing methods can be organized along two axes: what evidence is used to assess relevance, and how observations are selected given that evidence. On the evidence axis, prior approaches occupy two extremes. View-level heuristics such as timestamp proximity or field-of-view (FoV) overlap estimated from camera poses cite55†Xiao et al. (2025) ; cite56†Yu et al.\nL96: (2025b) are efficient but coarse: they reason at the granularity of whole views rather than pixels, and therefore cannot determine which target-view regions a candidate memory frame can actually support, nor can they account for occlusion. Reconstruction-based memory, in contrast, maintains an explicit geometric representation of the scene — a global scene model rendered for novel-view guidance cite77†Liu et al. (2024) ; cite78†Wang and Agapito (2025) ; cite79†Ren et al. (2025) ; cite80†Zhang et al.\nL97: (2025) , or a surfel-indexed view memory anchoring past views to observed surface elements cite57†Li et al. (2025) . While such representations provide fine-grained geometric evidence, they require constructing, optimizing, and incrementally updating a persistent scene model, a cost that compounds over long autoregressive rollouts and introduces failure modes when reconstruction is imperfect. On the selection axis, prior methods predominantly score candidates independently and retrieve the top-$n$.\nL98: Because relevance is assessed per frame in isolation, the resulting set is prone to redundancy; multiple retrieved frames may cover the same target-view region, while other regions remain unsupported. Under tight retrieval budgets imposed by the model’s context limits, this redundancy directly reduces geometric coverage.\nL99: ## 3 Preliminaries\nL100: \nL101: Notation. We use bold lowercase symbols (e.g., ${\\mathbf{x}}$) for video tensors, calligraphic symbols (e.g., $\\mathcal{C},\\mathcal{M},\\mathcal{H}$) for index sets, subscripts to indicate frame indices, and superscripts for diffusion noise level. Given an index set $\\mathcal{S}$, we write ${\\mathbf{x}}_{S}=\\{{\\mathbf{x}}_{j}\\}_{j\\in S}$ for the subset of frames indexed by $\\mathcal{S}$.\nL102: Autoregressive video diffusion. Let ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}=\\{{\\mathbf{x}}_{f}^{0}\\}_{f=1}^{F}$ denote a clean video with $F$ frames. Diffusion models generate ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}$ by starting from Gaussian noise and iteratively denoising through a learned reverse process parameterized by a network cite67†Ho et al. (2020) ; cite68†Song et al. (2021) ; cite81†Lipman et al. (2023) .\nL103: Using a continuous noise level $k\\in[0,1]$, where $k=1$ corresponds to pure noise and $k=0$ to the clean video, we write the noised sample as ${\\mathbf{x}}^{k}=\\alpha(k)\\,{\\mathbf{x}}^{0}+\\sigma(k)\\,\\epsilon$ with $\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\mathrm{I})$, where $\\alpha(k)$ and $\\sigma(k)$ are noise schedule functions satisfying $\\alpha(0)=1$, $\\sigma(0)=0$ and $\\alpha(1)=0$, $\\sigma(1)=1$. A common training objective is to learn a noise predictor $\\epsilon_{\\theta}$ (or score/velocity field) by minimizing\nL104:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\theta}=\\mathbb{E}_{{\\mathbf{x}}^{0},\\epsilon,k}\\Big[\\big\\|\\epsilon_{\\theta}({\\mathbf{x}}^{k},k)-\\epsilon\\big\\|^{2}\\Big].$$  |  | (1)\nL105: Autoregressive video diffusion extends this framework to long-horizon generation by denoising frame ${\\mathbf{x}}_{t}^{k}$ conditioned on a temporal context window of previously generated clean frames ${\\mathbf{x}}_{\\mathcal{C}_{t}}^{0}$, where $\\mathcal{C}_{t}=\\{t-1,\\ldots,t-\\delta\\}$ is the index set of the most recent $\\delta$ frames. Diffusion Forcing (DF) cite63†Song et al. (2025) ; cite72†Chen et al.\nL106: (2024) generalizes this formulation by assigning an independent noise level to each frame, allowing the model to denoise the current frame while conditioning on context frames at arbitrary noise levels.\nL107: Memory bank with retrieval. Let $\\mathcal{M}_{t}$ denote the index set of the memory bank at generation frame $t$, and similarly $\\mathcal{H}_{t}$ for retrieved indices. Memory-augmented video generators cite55†Xiao et al. (2025) ; cite56†Yu et al. (2025b) ; cite58†Chen et al. (2025) maintain an external memory bank ${\\mathbf{x}}_{\\mathcal{M}_{t}}$ at current frame $t$ that stores previously generated frames and associated metadata such as camera poses.\nL108: Before generating the next frame ${\\mathbf{x}}_{t}^{0}$, the model retrieves a subset of memory frames ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{H}_{t}}\\subseteq{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\mathcal{M}_{t}}^{0}$, and conditions the network on ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{H}_{t}}$, i.e., $\\epsilon_{\\theta}({\\mathbf{x}}^{k}_{t},\\,{\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{C}_{t}},\\,{\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{H}_{t}},\\,k).$\nL109: ## 4 Method\nL110: Our goal is to retrieve a compact set of historical frames ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{H}_{t}}\\subseteq{\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{M}_{t}}$ that provides useful geometric evidence for generating the target frame ${\\mathbf{x}}_{t}^{0}$. The key idea is to identify which regions of the target view are already geometrically supported by the current context, and then retrieve frames that support the remaining uncovered regions. Our method consists of three main components.\nL111: First, in Sec. cite10†4.1 , we construct target-view coverage maps by warping context and memory frames into the target viewpoint using estimated depth and camera poses. A target-view coverage map indicates which target pixels are geometrically supported by a given source frame.\nL112: Second, in Sec. cite11†4.2 , we select memory frames according to residual coverage gain, which measures how much additional target-view coverage a candidate frame provides beyond the current context and the already-selected memory frames. This encourages retrieved frames to be complementary rather than redundant. Finally, we introduce sliding-window depth caching for scalable long-horizon geometry estimation in Sec. cite12†4.3 .\nL113: ### 4.1 Target-view coverage maps as 3D memory evidence\nL114: CovRAG uses depth-based pixel-wise visibility as 3D-aware memory evidence. For each source frame $i$ and target frame $t$, we construct a target-view coverage map ${\\mathbf{m}}_{i\\rightarrow t}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{H\\times W}$, where $H$ and $W$ denote the frame height and width. The map ${\\mathbf{m}}_{i\\rightarrow t}$ indicates which pixels in the target frame $t$ are geometrically supported by the source frame $i$.\nL115: Let ${\\mathbf{d}}_{i}$ be the depth map of source frame $i$, and let ${\\mathbf{p}}_{i}=[\\mathbf{R}_{i}\\mid\\mathbf{t}_{i}]$ and ${\\mathbf{p}}_{t}=[\\mathbf{R}_{t}\\mid\\mathbf{t}_{t}]$ denote the camera poses of the source and target views, respectively. For each pixel $\\mathbf{u}$ in frame $i$, we back-project it to 3D using its depth ${\\mathbf{d}}_{i}(\\mathbf{u})$, transform the resulting 3D point into the target camera coordinate system, and project it onto the target image plane.\nL116: If the projected point lands on a valid target pixel $\\mathbf{v}$, we mark that pixel as covered:\nL117:  | $${\\mathbf{m}}_{i\\rightarrow t}(\\mathbf{v})=1.$$  |  | (2)\nL118: Specifically, we round each projected coordinate to its nearest target pixel v and mark ${\\mathbf{v}}$ as covered if ${\\mathbf{v}}\\in[1,H]\\times[1,W]$. Otherwise, the target pixel remains uncovered, i.e., ${\\mathbf{m}}_{i\\rightarrow t}(\\mathbf{v})=0$. In practice, multiple source pixels may project to the same target pixel; we mark a target pixel as covered if at least one source pixel lands on it.\nL119: For the current context window $\\mathcal{C}_{t}$, we aggregate the coverage maps of all context frames into a single context coverage map:\nL120:  | $${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}=\\bigvee_{i\\in\\mathcal{C}_{t}}{\\mathbf{m}}_{i\\rightarrow t},$$  |  | (3)\nL121: where $\\vee$ denotes the element-wise logical OR operator. This map indicates which target-view pixels are already covered by the recent temporal context. The remaining uncovered pixels define the regions where additional historical memory evidence may be useful. The target-view coverage map provides a lightweight but spatially fine-grained form of memory evidence. Unlike FoV overlap, it distinguishes which pixels of the target view are supported by a source frame.\nL122: Moreover, it requires neither a maintained global scene representation nor the per-step rendering required by explicit-reconstruction methods. Thus, depth-based coverage maps provide a practical middle ground: they are sufficiently detailed for target-view visibility reasoning while remaining lightweight enough.\nL123: ### 4.2 Residual coverage gain for non-redundant retrieval\nL124: \nL125: Algorithm 1 Coverage-Maximizing Retrieval\nL126: \nL127: 1:  Require: Temporal context ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{C}_{t}}$, memory ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{M}_{t}}$, target pose ${\\mathbf{p}}_{t}$, retrieval budget $n$\nL128: \nL129: 2:  Compute ${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}$ by Eq. cite82†3 L130: \nL131: 3:  Initialize $\\mathcal{H}_{t}=\\emptyset$\nL132: \nL133: 4:  for $\\ell=1$ to $n$ do\nL134: \nL135: 5:   for all $j\\in\\mathcal{M}_{t}\\setminus\\mathcal{H}_{t}$ do\nL136: 6:    Compute ${\\mathbf{m}}_{{j}\\rightarrow t}$ by Eq. cite83†2 L137: \nL138: 7:    Compute $g_{j}$ by Eq. cite84†4 L139: \nL140: 8:   end for\nL141: \nL142: 9:   Retrieve frame $j^{\\star}$ by Eq. cite85†5 L143: \nL144: 10:   Update $\\mathcal{H}_{t}$ by $\\mathcal{H}_{t}\\cup\\{j^{\\star}\\}$\nL145: \nL146: 11:   Update ${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}$ by Eq. cite86†6 L147: \nL148: 12:  end for\nL149: \nL150: 13:  return Retrieved frames ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{\\mathcal{H}_{t}}$\nL151: Given target-view coverage maps, the next question is how to select memory frames. A natural strategy, adopted by methods such as WorldMem cite55†Xiao et al. (2025) , is to score each candidate frame independently by its total coverage of the target view and retrieve the top-$n$ frames. However, this can select redundant frames that cover the same target-view regions, leaving other target-view regions uncovered.\nL152: Since the number of retrieved frames is limited by the conditioning budget and computational cost, retrieval should favor frames that provide complementary evidence. To this end, CovRAG selects frames according to residual coverage gain.\nL153: Residual coverage gain. For each candidate memory frame $j\\in\\mathcal{M}_{t}$, we compute its residual coverage gain as\nL154: \nL155:  | $$g_{j}=\\sum_{\\mathbf{v}}\\left(1-{\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}(\\mathbf{v})\\right){\\mathbf{m}}_{j\\rightarrow t}(\\mathbf{v}),$$  |  | (4)\nL156: where ${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}$ denotes the current context coverage map and ${\\mathbf{m}}_{j\\rightarrow t}$ denotes the target-view coverage map computed for candidate frame $j$. Pixels already covered by the context, i.e., ${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}(\\mathbf{v})=1$, do not contribute to the score. Thus, $g_{j}$ counts the number of currently uncovered target-view pixels that would become covered if frame $j$ were retrieved.\nL157: A higher $g_{j}$ indicates that frame j contributes more new geometric coverage; a $g_{j}$ of zero indicates complete redundancy with already covered regions.\nL158: Retrieval by maximizing residual coverage gain. We iteratively select $n$ memory frames that maximize residual coverage gain. We initialize the retrieved set as $\\mathcal{H}_{t}=\\emptyset$ and compute the initial context coverage map ${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}$ from the current context window. At each iteration, we choose the candidate with the largest residual coverage gain:\nL159: \nL160:  | $$j^{\\star}=\\arg\\max_{j\\in\\mathcal{M}_{t}\\setminus\\mathcal{H}_{t}}g_{j},$$  |  | (5)\nL161: and add it to the retrieved set, $\\mathcal{H}_{t}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{H}_{t}\\cup\\{j^{\\star}\\}$. We then update the context coverage map by incorporating the newly selected frame:\nL162: \nL163:  | $${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}\\leftarrow{\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}\\vee{\\mathbf{m}}_{j^{\\star}\\rightarrow t}.$$  |  | (6)\nL164: After the update, we recompute $g_{j}$ for the remaining candidates using the updated context coverage map ${\\mathbf{m}}^{\\mathrm{ctx}}_{t}$ and repeat this process until $|\\mathcal{H}_{t}|=n$. By selecting frames according to residual coverage gain, CovRAG explicitly discourages redundancy among retrieved memories. Each selected frame is encouraged to cover target-view regions that are not already covered by the current context or by previously selected frames.\nL165: The resulting set, therefore, provides a compact, complementary set of memory frames that collectively covers the target view. Algorithm cite87†1 summarizes the procedure for retrieving $n$ historical frames for generating the target frame $t$.\nL166: ### 4.3 Scalable depth estimation via sliding-window caching\nL167: We assume access to a per-frame depth map $\\mathbf{d}_{i}$ for each source frame in Eq. cite83†2 . We obtain these depths using a pretrained multi-view geometry model, VGGT cite62†Wang et al. (2025a) , which predicts per-frame depth and relative camera motion from a set of input views. However, applying VGGT to the entire generated history is impractical for long autoregressive rollouts, since the number of frames grows over time and VGGT is designed to process only a limited number of views.\nL168: To make depth-based coverage estimation scalable, we introduce a sliding-window depth caching strategy.\nL169: Let $L$ denote the window size. At generation step $t$, we run VGGT only on the most recent window $\\mathcal{W}_{t}=\\{{\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{t-L+1},\\ldots,{\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{t}\\}$, and obtain depth maps $\\{{\\mathbf{d}}_{i}\\}_{i\\in\\mathcal{W}_{t}}$. Because consecutive windows overlap, previously generated frames are included together with newly generated frames, providing local multi-view context for depth estimation across adjacent steps. We then cache the depth maps of newly generated frames.\nL170: When a frame $j$ enters the memory bank $\\mathcal{M}_{t}$, its depth map ${\\mathbf{d}}_{j}$ is stored together with its RGB frame ${\\mathbf{x}}^{0}_{j}$ and camera pose ${\\mathbf{p}}_{j}$. Later, when computing the target-view coverage map ${\\mathbf{m}}_{j\\rightarrow t}$ for a candidate memory frame $j$, CovRAG reuses the cached depth ${\\mathbf{d}}_{j}$ and warps the frame into the target pose ${\\mathbf{p}}_{t}$.\nL171: This strategy keeps geometry estimation efficient during long-horizon generation. VGGT is evaluated only on a fixed-size local window, so the per-step geometry estimation cost remains bounded by $L$, rather than growing with the length of the generated video. At the same time, cached depths allow historical frames to be reused for target-view coverage computation throughout the autoregressive rollout, without repeatedly re-estimating geometry for the full memory bank.\nL172: ## 5 Experiments\nL173: \nL174: Datasets.\nL175: \nL176: cite88†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Qualitative comparison on DL3DV10K. We compare 100-frame autoregressive rollouts from WorldMem, VMem, CovRAG, and the ground-truth video on DL3DV10K trajectories.\nL177: We evaluate CovRAG on RealEstate10K cite64†Zhou et al. (2018) and DL3DV10K cite65†Ling et al. (2024) . Following the DFoT cite63†Song et al. (2025) setup, we resize all frames to $256\\times 256$. For RealEstate10K, we construct a custom loop-closing evaluation protocol to explicitly test long-horizon revisitation consistency, where the camera trajectory returns to its initial pose after a long temporal gap.\nL178: For DL3DV10K, we use the original trajectories, as the dataset already contains diverse scenes and camera motions that explore larger spatial extents over longer horizons. Details of dataset construction and trajectory visualizations are provided in the supplementary material.\nL179: Implementation details. We build our method on top of the Diffusion Forcing Transformer (DFoT) cite63†Song et al. (2025) . For RealEstate10K cite64†Zhou et al. (2018) , we initialize from the DFoT checkpoint pretrained on RealEstate10K and attach a memory-attention module following WorldMem cite55†Xiao et al. (2025) . We freeze the DFoT backbone and train only the memory conditioning module for 50K steps on 8 H100 GPUs with a batch size of 64. For DL3DV10K cite65†Ling et al.\nL180: (2024) , we first adapt the RealEstate10K pretrained DFoT by finetuning it on DL3DV10K for 80K steps on 8 GPUs with a batch size of 48. We then train the memory-augmented model on DL3DV10K with full finetuning for 80K steps with a batch size of 32. At inference time, we retrieve $n=2$ memory frames at each generation step. We use a temporal context window of $\\delta=5$ for RealEstate10K and $\\delta=2$ for DL3DV10K. Additional training and evaluation details are provided in the supplementary material.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nI3DM: Implicit 3D-aware Memory Retrieval and Injection for Consistent Video Scene Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2603.23413v2)\nciteturn247view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2603.23413v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 532\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn247view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://demuc.de/papers/schoenberger2016mvs.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"

